```java
import java.io.*;

public class Secure {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            File file = new File("path/to/file.txt");
            FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream(file);
            // Secure code here
        } catch (FileNotFoundException e) {
            System.out.println("Error: File not found.");
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.out.println("Error: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

In the bad code snippet, the `FileInputStream` is not properly closed, which can lead to resource leaks and vulnerabilities. In the good code snippet, the `FileInputStream` is properly closed using a `finally` block, ensuring that resources are released even if an exception occurs.

To fix the bad code snippet, you can use a try-with-resources statement, which automatically closes the resources, even if an exception occurs.

```java
import java.io.*;

public class Secure {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try (FileInputStream fis = new FileInputStream("path/to/file.txt")) {
            // Secure code here
        } catch (FileNotFoundException e) {
            System.out.println("Error: File not found.");
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.out.println("Error: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

In this fixed code snippet, the `FileInputStream` is properly closed using a try-with-resources statement, ensuring that resources are released even if an exception occurs.

